  "slug": "mortgage-broker-vs-lender",
  "metaTitle": "Mortgage Broker vs Lender: How Each Works and Gets Paid",
  "metaDescription": "Mortgage broker vs lender compared: who makes the loan, how each gets paid, who pays the fee, and what to ask before you sign with either one.",
  "h1": "Mortgage Broker vs Lender: Using a Broker or Going Straight to a Mortgage Lender",
  "subtitle": "A side-by-side look at what a mortgage broker and a mortgage lender each do and how each one gets paid for your loan.",
  "optionAName": "Mortgage Broker",
  "optionBName": "Mortgage Lender",
  "introText": "A mortgage lender makes your loan, and a mortgage broker helps you find one. The lender is the financial institution you repay, while the broker does not lend money and is paid a loan-specific fee for its services. At ModernWallet, we publish plain-language money guides and free calculators, and this mortgage broker vs lender comparison sticks to what the [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB)](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-the-difference-between-a-mortgage-lender-and-a-mortgage-broker-en-130/) publishes about each role.\n\nNeither path is automatically better. Going straight to a lender gets you that one institution's offer, and hiring a broker gets you someone who typically works with multiple lenders. Whichever you pick, the CFPB says to shop around for the best loan terms and the lowest interest rates and fees.",
  "comparisonTable": {
